<xml>
  <block type="device_servo" x="25" y="25">
    <mutation uid="scanpan" />
    <field name="NAME">pan</field>
    <field name="PIN">9</field>
    <field name="DEVICE_HUE">210</field>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Purpose: Scan a photocell on A0 with two servos. Send ordinary Serial text: one @image header, then @pixel x,y,value rows. Plotter stores the image on the computer.
Hardware: use Connections to check pin ownership for your selected board. Open the comment bubbles on the main steps for explanations.</comment>
  </block>
  <block type="device_servo" x="385" y="25">
    <mutation uid="scantilt" />
    <field name="NAME">tilt</field>
    <field name="PIN">10</field>
    <field name="DEVICE_HUE">30</field>
  </block>
  <block type="serial_config" x="745" y="25">
    <field name="BAUD">115200</field>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_setup_section" x="25" y="180">
    <statement name="DO">
      <block type="serial_text">
        <field name="TEXT">@image {"version":1,"id":"photocell","width":64,"height":48,"mode":"gray","max":1023}</field>
        <field name="END">println</field>
        <comment pinned="false">Ordinary Serial text. Plotter recognises this header and creates a 64×48 image. Change its JSON in the message editor; the Arduino needs no JSON library. Sent once before pixel data.</comment>
      </block>
    </statement>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Runs once after power-on or reset. Initialise the program here; repeating measurements and animation belong in loop.</comment>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_loop_section" x="25" y="340">
    <statement name="DO">
      <block type="controls_for">
        <field name="VAR">y</field>
        <value name="FROM">
          <block type="math_number">
            <field name="NUM">0</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="TO">
          <block type="math_number">
            <field name="NUM">47</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <value name="BY">
          <block type="math_number">
            <field name="NUM">1</field>
          </block>
        </value>
        <statement name="DO">
          <block type="controls_for">
            <field name="VAR">x</field>
            <value name="FROM">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">0</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <value name="TO">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">63</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <value name="BY">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">1</field>
              </block>
            </value>
            <statement name="DO">
              <block type="device_servo_write">
                <field name="DEVICE">scanpan</field>
                <value name="ANGLE">
                  <block type="base_map">
                    <value name="NUM">
                      <block type="variables_get">
                        <field name="VAR">x</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="FROM_LOW">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">0</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="FROM_HIGH">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">63</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="TO_LOW">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">35</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="DMAX">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">145</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Convert a value from one numeric range to another. All five inputs can be expressions. Arduino map uses integer arithmetic and does not clamp values outside the source range.</comment>
                  </block>
                </value>
                <next>
                  <block type="device_servo_write">
                    <field name="DEVICE">scantilt</field>
                    <value name="ANGLE">
                      <block type="base_map">
                        <value name="NUM">
                          <block type="variables_get">
                            <field name="VAR">y</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <value name="FROM_LOW">
                          <block type="math_number">
                            <field name="NUM">0</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <value name="FROM_HIGH">
                          <block type="math_number">
                            <field name="NUM">47</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <value name="TO_LOW">
                          <block type="math_number">
                            <field name="NUM">45</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <value name="DMAX">
                          <block type="math_number">
                            <field name="NUM">135</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Convert a value from one numeric range to another. All five inputs can be expressions. Arduino map uses integer arithmetic and does not clamp values outside the source range.</comment>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <next>
                      <block type="base_delay">
                        <value name="DELAY_TIME">
                          <block type="math_number">
                            <field name="NUM">20</field>
                          </block>
                        </value>
                        <next>
                          <block type="serial_text">
                            <field name="TEXT">@pixel </field>
                            <field name="END">print</field>
                            <next>
                              <block type="serial_send">
                                <field name="END">print</field>
                                <value name="VALUE">
                                  <block type="variables_get">
                                    <field name="VAR">x</field>
                                  </block>
                                </value>
                                <next>
                                  <block type="serial_text">
                                    <field name="TEXT">,</field>
                                    <field name="END">print</field>
                                    <next>
                                      <block type="serial_send">
                                        <field name="END">print</field>
                                        <value name="VALUE">
                                          <block type="variables_get">
                                            <field name="VAR">y</field>
                                          </block>
                                        </value>
                                        <next>
                                          <block type="serial_text">
                                            <field name="TEXT">,</field>
                                            <field name="END">print</field>
                                            <next>
                                              <block type="serial_send">
                                                <field name="END">println</field>
                                                <value name="VALUE">
                                                  <block type="inout_analog_read">
                                                    <field name="PIN">A0</field>
                                                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Measure the voltage on this analog input. On ATmega168/328, the ADC returns 0–1023; other boards may use a different ADC range. A potentiometer wiper or analog sensor connects here.</comment>
                                                  </block>
                                                </value>
                                                <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Send this value/text to the computer. A new line finishes one record; same line can build a record from several values. Match Monitor/Plotter baud to the sketch.</comment>
                                              </block>
                                            </next>
                                            <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Send this value/text to the computer. A new line finishes one record; same line can build a record from several values. Match Monitor/Plotter baud to the sketch.</comment>
                                          </block>
                                        </next>
                                        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Send this value/text to the computer. A new line finishes one record; same line can build a record from several values. Match Monitor/Plotter baud to the sketch.</comment>
                                      </block>
                                    </next>
                                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Send this value/text to the computer. A new line finishes one record; same line can build a record from several values. Match Monitor/Plotter baud to the sketch.</comment>
                                  </block>
                                </next>
                                <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Send this value/text to the computer. A new line finishes one record; same line can build a record from several values. Match Monitor/Plotter baud to the sketch.</comment>
                              </block>
                            </next>
                            <comment pinned="false">Send one text line: @pixel x,y,brightness. Commas separate the values; only the last send ends the line. The PC stores the image and overwrites just this pixel. No image buffer lives on the Arduino.</comment>
                          </block>
                        </next>
                        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Wait for the given number of milliseconds (1000 ms = 1 second). This pauses the main program; avoid long waits when loop must service motors, music files or serial input.</comment>
                      </block>
                    </next>
                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Send a target position to the named servo. The library keeps generating pulses; reaching the target takes mechanical time. Position mode uses degrees, pulse mode uses microseconds.</comment>
                  </block>
                </next>
                <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Send a target position to the named servo. The library keeps generating pulses; reaching the target takes mechanical time. Position mode uses degrees, pulse mode uses microseconds.</comment>
              </block>
            </statement>
            <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Repeat the body with x taking each value from the start to the end, using the given step. Array indices start at zero.</comment>
          </block>
        </statement>
        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Repeat the body with y taking each value from the start to the end, using the given step. Array indices start at zero.</comment>
      </block>
    </statement>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Repeats until power is removed. Each block runs in order, then execution returns to the top. Keep this loop responsive when servicing motors, music or serial input.</comment>
  </block>
</xml>
